{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000001","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Agent Supervision","description":"Guidelines for supervising and coordinating sub-agents in Minerva.","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-08T05:00:00","tags":["agent-supervision","workflow"],"preconditions":"DocketManager loaded, at least one project docket open.","steps":"## Setup\n1. Use minerva_list_models (NO provider filter) to see ALL available providers and enum IDs.\n2. Choose the right provider for each worker. Pass provider= to spawn_worker. IMPORTANT: 'chatgpt' and 'openai' are DIFFERENT providers. If the user says 'ChatGPT', use provider='chatgpt'.\n3. For GPT/ChatGPT workers, ALWAYS pass skills= for static tool mode — they cannot do dynamic discovery. Example: skills=[\"Co-Browser Automation\"].\n4. For Gemini/Claude workers, skills= is optional but still saves rounds.\n\n## Spawning\n5. Spawn all workers FIRST, then monitor. Do not spawn one, wait, then spawn the next.\n6. Give each worker a COMPLETE task description. Include everything it needs to know — the worker cannot see your conversation or ask you questions.\n\n## Monitoring — HANDS OFF\n7. After spawning, do your own setup work (create spreadsheet, etc.) before checking workers. Workers need time to run.\n8. Use minerva_check_worker to monitor (cheap, ~100 tokens). When checking multiple workers, batch ALL check_worker calls into ONE parallel tool round.\n9. Check each worker AT MOST twice before it completes. Do NOT rapid-fire poll. If a worker is still running, do something else and check back later.\n10. NEVER call minerva_get_chat_history on a running worker. After status='completed', call minerva_get_chat_history once to get the metadata list, then hydrate only the specific messages you need with minerva_get_chat_messages.\n11. NEVER call task-specific tools yourself (cobrowser, file tools, etc.). Your job is to COORDINATE, not to DO the work. If a worker fails, send guidance or spawn a replacement — do not duplicate its work.\n\n## Communication\n12. NEVER send a message to a worker with status='completed'. Sending restarts it, wasting budget. Read its output and move on.\n13. Only use minerva_send_message if a worker is stuck (status='running' but rounds_used hasn't changed across checks). Keep messages short and actionable.\n\n## Finishing\n14. Once ALL workers have completed: read each worker's metadata list ONCE via minerva_get_chat_history, hydrate only the final assistant message or other specific indices you need via minerva_get_chat_messages, populate output (spreadsheet, etc.), and STOP.\n15. Do NOT hydrate all messages unless the user explicitly needs the full transcript. Completion notifications already include the worker's last response preview, which may be enough.\n16. Present results to the user exactly ONCE. Do not re-read transcripts, re-check workers, or regenerate your summary.\n\n## Spreadsheets\n17. If the user asks for a spreadsheet: minerva_tool_search('spreadsheet'), create with minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor, populate with minerva_update_spreadsheet_data.","outcome":"Work is decomposed and completed through coordinated sub-agents with minimal supervisor overhead.","tool_deps":["minerva_spawn_worker","minerva_list_workers","minerva_check_worker","minerva_approve_workers","minerva_send_message","minerva_get_chat_history","minerva_get_chat_messages","minerva_list_models","minerva_list_chats"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000002","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Knowledge Management","description":"How to use docket hints, insights, and quality scoring to build persistent knowledge.","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","tags":["hints","knowledge","tool-usage"],"preconditions":"Docket tools available (minerva_tool_search 'docket hint' to activate).","steps":"1. Before starting work on a component, check for existing knowledge: docket_hint_query(component=<name>).\n2. If hints exist, read them and apply. Bump useful hints with docket_quality(score=+3).\n3. During work, when you discover something non-obvious (a gotcha, working command, selector, pattern), save it immediately: docket_hint_set(component, key, value, tags).\n4. If you discover a mental model correction (you assumed X but Y is true), create an insight: docket_create(type=insight, assumed=X, corrected=Y).\n5. If knowledge caused errors or was outdated, downgrade it: docket_quality(score=-3, reason='outdated').\n6. Keep hint values short and actionable — one-liners, small JSON, exact commands.\n7. Use component names that match the system being worked on (e.g., 'godot', 'docket', 'cobrowser').","outcome":"Knowledge accumulates across sessions. Future agents benefit from past discoveries without re-learning.","tool_deps":["minerva_docket_hint_set","minerva_docket_hint_get","minerva_docket_hint_query","minerva_docket_quality"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000003","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Tool Usage Patterns","description":"Efficient patterns for using Minerva's MCP tools.","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","tags":["efficiency","patterns","tool-usage"],"preconditions":"Minerva MCP tools connected.","steps":"1. Tools are auto-activated when you call minerva_get_skill — check the response for activated_tools. Do NOT call minerva_tool_search for tools that were already auto-activated.\n2. Only use minerva_tool_search for tools NOT covered by a skill you loaded, or when a tool call fails with \"not loaded\".\n3. For spreadsheets: minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor to create, minerva_update_spreadsheet_data to populate (csv_content for bulk, cells for individual updates).\n4. For terminal work: minerva_terminal_create, then minerva_terminal_write with \\r for Enter (not \\n).\n5. For webviews: minerva_create_webview_panel with HTML.\n6. For notes: minerva_create_note for persistent context.\n7. Prefer targeted queries over broad fetches. If a result is truncated, narrow your query.\n8. Complete tasks in as few tool calls as possible while being thorough.\n9. Tools stay activated for the entire session — do NOT re-search for tools you already have.","outcome":"Efficient tool usage with minimal wasted calls."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000004","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Docket Work Tracking","description":"How to use docket for tracking and managing work items.","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","tags":["docket","tool-suite","work-tracking"],"preconditions":"At least one project docket loaded. Activate tools via minerva_tool_search('docket').","steps":"1. Activate docket tools: minerva_tool_search('docket create') or minerva_tool_search('docket query').\n2. Create work items with clear titles and descriptions: docket_create(type=work_item, title=..., description=...).\n3. Use parent fields to build hierarchies: docket_create(parent=<parent_id>).\n4. Follow state machines — work_item: backlog → open → in_progress → done.\n5. Use docket_transition to move items through states. Include a note explaining why.\n6. Use docket_query to find items by type, status, tags, or text search.\n7. Use docket_comment to add discussion to items.\n8. Use docket_link to create relationships (blocks, caused_by, follow_up).\n9. For bugs: type=bug, states: new → triaged → active → resolved → verified → closed.\n10. For design changes: type=dcr, states: proposed → approved → designing → implementing → reviewing → shipped.","outcome":"Work is tracked with full history, relationships, and state machine enforcement.","tool_deps":["minerva_docket_create","minerva_docket_query","minerva_docket_transition","minerva_docket_update","minerva_docket_comment","minerva_docket_get"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000005","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Co-Browser Automation","description":"Efficient web browsing and data extraction with cobrowser tools. Core pattern: navigate, verify with page_identity, extract with page_section fields maps, commit results immediately.","created_at":"2026-04-05T08:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-12T15:30:00-07:00","tags":["cobrowser","tool-suite","web-automation"],"preconditions":"Cobrowser MCP server connected, Firefox extension active.","steps":"1. Create a browser tab with cobrowser_tab_new(). This returns a tab_id for all subsequent commands.\n2. Navigate directly to the target URL with cobrowser_navigate. For JS-heavy sites (Amazon, Google, SPAs), use delay=2 or delay=3 to allow page hydration. Do NOT navigate to a homepage first unless the task requires it.\n3. After EVERY navigation, call cobrowser_page_identity. Verify the page type, title, and URL match expectations. If wrong, try ONE recovery (direct navigate or targeted click). If still wrong, cobrowser_request_human.\n4. If cobrowser_navigate returned _injected_knowledge (site-specific selector hints), read them BEFORE building your first fields map. Use the injected selectors exactly as given — they were verified on this site. Do NOT guess alternative selectors when hints are provided.\n5. For pages with repeating items (search results, product cards, table rows), use cobrowser_page_section with a fields map on the PARENT CONTAINER — the element that WRAPS all the repeating items, NOT the item selector itself. To find the parent container: use page_structure or query_all to identify the repeated item pattern, then target its parent wrapper (common patterns: main, [role='main'], .search-results, .results-list). Do NOT use the individual item selector — that matches one item, not all of them. Example: page_section(selector='.results-list', fields={'title': 'h2', 'price': '.price', 'url': 'a@href'}). Append @attribute to extract attributes instead of text (e.g. 'a@href' for links, 'img@src' for images). This is the primary extraction tool — one call can return all items on a page.\n6. If fields extraction returns 0 items or missing data, do NOT retry with the same selectors and do NOT fall back to per-item scraping. Instead: (a) call page_section on ONE individual result element WITHOUT the fields parameter to see its raw HTML structure — read the text, links, and buttons output to identify the correct CSS selectors, (b) retry the fields extraction on the PARENT CONTAINER with corrected selectors. This is a 2-call recovery, not a per-item loop.\n7. If @attribute extraction (e.g. a@href, img@src) returns null in a fields map, do NOT fabricate URLs or data. Instead, call page_section WITHOUT fields on one element — the links and buttons output always includes hrefs. Use those to build correct selectors or extract data directly.\n8. Use cobrowser_query_all to discover elements, count matches, and get xpaths. It returns metadata (tag, class, text snippet, xpath) per element. Use it when you need to find container selectors, verify element counts, or get xpaths for subsequent targeted reads. Note: query_all text snippets often contain enough data (names, prices, ratings) to complete the task without per-item reads. Check the snippets before drilling into individual elements.\n9. NEVER fabricate, estimate, or fill in data that was not in the extraction results. If a field is null, partial, or missing, report it as-is or mark it unavailable. Do NOT guess prices, invent URLs, or pad incomplete values. Let the user decide what to do with gaps.\n10. Once you have structured results, commit them to the output immediately (spreadsheet, note, or response). Do NOT re-extract the same page unless a tool call visibly failed. Do NOT search tool call history for data you already have in context.\n11. For detail pages: only navigate to individual items if the listing page lacks data you need. After drilling in, use page_identity to verify, then a targeted page_section read.\n12. cobrowser_scroll: only when content is below the fold or the page uses infinite scroll.\n13. cobrowser_click / cobrowser_type: only for required interactions — filters, forms, pagination, login.\n14. cobrowser_page_structure: escalation tool only. Use when page_identity and page_section are insufficient to understand page layout.\n15. CSS selector gotcha: element IDs with hyphens, dots, or other special characters (common on Amazon, Google, and SPAs) are invalid as bare CSS ID selectors. Do NOT use #uuid-with-hyphens. Instead use attribute selectors like [data-cel-widget='search_result_2'] or [id='uuid-with-hyphens'], or use xpath. When query_all returns xpaths, prefer those over constructing CSS selectors from IDs.\n16. AVOID cobrowser_get_state (returns entire DOM, very expensive), cobrowser_screenshot (slow, rarely needed), cobrowser_tab_claim (multi-agent coordination only), cobrowser_native_* (OS-level dialogs only).\n17. If a tool call returns a POLICY BLOCK, read the error message and follow its instructions. A policy block means a required precondition was not met — it does NOT mean the tool is unavailable. Do NOT call minerva_tool_search for a tool that was policy-blocked.\n18. Budget: aim for 5-10 cobrowser calls per task. If you are past 15, stop and reconsider your approach.","outcome":"Efficient browsing with bulk extraction via fields maps, minimal per-item calls, and clear recovery patterns.","tool_deps":["cobrowser_tab_new","cobrowser_navigate","cobrowser_page_identity","cobrowser_page_structure","cobrowser_page_section","cobrowser_query_all","cobrowser_click","cobrowser_type","cobrowser_scroll","cobrowser_request_human"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000006","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Document Tools","description":"Guidelines for buffer-based document read/edit/save tools.","created_at":"2026-04-05T08:00:00","updated_at":"2026-06-03T00:00:00","tags":["documents","tool-suite"],"preconditions":"Document buffer tools are core (always available). File search and shell require the optional codetools plugin.","steps":"1. Activate document tools: minerva_tool_search('doc read').\n2. Use minerva_doc_read with offset/limit to read only the sections you need (path-canonical, routes through the document buffer).\n3. For targeted edits, call minerva_doc_edit with a unique old_string — the buffer is updated in memory, NOT on disk.\n4. To replace a file's contents wholesale, use minerva_doc_write — also buffer-only.\n5. Flush buffer changes to disk with minerva_doc_save (single path) or minerva_doc_save_all (every dirty buffer). Until save, disk is unchanged.\n6. File globbing, content search, and shell commands are NOT core tools — install the optional `codetools` marketplace plugin, which provides minerva_codetools_glob / minerva_codetools_grep / minerva_codetools_bash.\n7. If a tool times out or fails, try a different approach rather than retrying the same thing.\n8. If results are truncated, use more specific queries to get the data you need.","outcome":"Targeted document operations with minimal wasted reads.","tool_deps":["minerva_doc_read","minerva_doc_write","minerva_doc_edit","minerva_doc_save","minerva_doc_save_all"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000010","type":"prompt","status":"active","title":"Agentic Base","description":"Base system prompt for agentic mode. Bootstraps tool search and skill discovery.","created_at":"2026-04-05T08:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-13T03:30:00","tags":["agentic","system-prompt"],"component":"system-prompt","key":"agentic-base","prompt_text":"You are an AI assistant with access to tools.\n\nPhase 1 — SET UP (do this first):\n1. Call minerva_list_skills to see available guides.\n2. If a skill description says it covers multiple areas (e.g. \"covers BOTH browsing and spreadsheet output — no need to load separate skills\"), load ONLY that skill. Do not load additional skills for areas it already covers.\n3. Call minerva_get_skill for each relevant skill. This auto-activates the tools you need — do NOT call minerva_tool_search for tools that a skill already activated.\n4. Only use minerva_tool_search for tools not covered by a skill.\n\nPhase 2 — EXECUTE:\nOnce your tools are ready, do the task. Do not search for more tools during execution."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000020","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Amazon DOM selector patterns","description":"Verified selector patterns for Amazon product search pages. Discovered through trace analysis of cobrowser automation sessions.","created_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dom","selectors"],"value":"Product URLs: use a.a-link-normal@href (NOT h2 a@href — Amazon wraps h2 inside a, so h2 a matches nothing). Prices: use .a-offscreen inside .a-price for full price text (e.g. '$12.99'). Parent container for search results: .s-main-slot (wraps all result cards). CSS ID gotcha: Amazon uses UUIDs with hyphens as element IDs — use [id='...'] attribute selector, not #id. Sponsored label: check for 'Sponsored' text in query_all snippets to filter ads.","component":"cobrowser","key":"amazon-selectors","target":"sonnet"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000021","type":"policy","status":"active","title":"Inject Amazon selector hints on navigation","description":"---policy-rule---\n{\"triggers\": [{\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_navigate\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}, {\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_tab_new\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}], \"effect\": \"inject\", \"priority\": 90, \"activate_scope\": \"amazon-selectors-injected\", \"scope_max_actions\": 100, \"scope_ttl_ms\": 2147483647, \"context_predicates\": {\"scope_active\": \"amazon-kb-read\", \"scope_not_active\": \"amazon-selectors-injected\"}, \"cooldown\": null, \"provenance\": \"user\", \"knowledge_ref\": \"019d5c00000000000000000000000020\", \"alternatives\": []}\n---end-rule---\n\nFires AFTER the Amazon KB block has been satisfied (requires amazon-kb-read scope active). Injects Amazon-specific DOM selector patterns into the cobrowser_navigate result so the LLM has correct selectors from the first extraction attempt. Activates amazon-selectors-injected scope to prevent re-injection on subsequent navigations. Priority 90 (below the block at 100).","created_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","inject","selectors"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000030","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Amazon Shopping Comparison","description":"End-to-end Amazon product comparison: search, extract, and build spreadsheet. Covers BOTH browsing and spreadsheet output — no need to load separate skills. Narrow tool set, low token footprint.","created_at":"2026-04-13T03:15:00","updated_at":"2026-04-13T04:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","comparison","shopping","spreadsheet"],"preconditions":"Cobrowser MCP server connected, Firefox extension active.","steps":"1. Call cobrowser_tab_new to open a browser tab.\n2. Navigate to Amazon with cobrowser_navigate. Use &s=review-rank (Avg. Customer Review) or &s=exact-aware-popularity-rank (Best Sellers) sort. Use delay=3 for JS hydration.\n3. If navigate returns _injected_knowledge, read the selector hints BEFORE extraction. Use the injected selectors exactly as given.\n4. Verify with cobrowser_page_identity — confirm it says Amazon search results.\n5. Extract with cobrowser_page_section(selector='.s-main-slot', fields={...}). Use the injected selectors for field keys. If no injected hints, use: name='h2 span', price='.a-offscreen', rating='.a-icon-alt', url='a.a-link-normal@href'.\n6. Use cobrowser_query_all to count results and check text snippets for review counts and Sponsored labels. Skip items with 'Sponsored' text or /sspa/ URLs.\n7. If fields return null for a column, do ONE recovery: cobrowser_page_section on a single result WITHOUT fields to see raw HTML structure. Fix selectors and retry on .s-main-slot. Do NOT do per-item extraction loops.\n8. Once you have 6+ organic results with name/price/URL, STOP extracting. Commit to spreadsheet immediately.\n9. Build CSV: no commas in values (strip them), prices as plain numbers (9.99), counts as integers (79900). Only include columns with data.\n10. Create spreadsheet: minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor with csv_content.\n11. Format: minerva_format_cells header row (A1:{last_col}1, bg=#2C3E50, text=#FFFFFF, bold). Then minerva_set_column_width. Then minerva_format_cells currency on price column.\n12. Verify: minerva_get_spreadsheet_data to confirm data is correct.\n13. Flag products priced 2x+ above commodity anchor as Premium-priced in a Notes column.\n14. Budget: 5-7 cobrowser calls, 3-4 spreadsheet calls. If past 10 cobrowser calls, stop and reconsider.","outcome":"Spreadsheet with product comparison: name, price, rating, reviews, notes, URL for 6 organic results.","tool_deps":["cobrowser_tab_new","cobrowser_navigate","cobrowser_page_identity","cobrowser_page_section","cobrowser_query_all","minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor","minerva_get_spreadsheet_data","minerva_format_cells","minerva_set_column_width"],"optimization":{"context_window":8,"summary_mode":"deterministic","tool_budget":3000,"tool_idle_turns":0}}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000031","type":"kb","status":"active","title":"Skill Authoring Guide","description":"Reference for creating and tuning Minerva skills. Covers fields, optimization profiles, and best practices.","created_at":"2026-04-13T04:30:00","updated_at":"2026-04-13T04:30:00","tags":["authoring","optimization","reference","skills"],"component":"skills","key":"skill-authoring-guide","summary":"How to write effective Minerva skills: field reference, optimization knobs, narrow vs broad design, and default values.","article":"## Skill Fields\n\n### Required\n- **title**: Short, descriptive name. Include the domain and action (e.g. \"Amazon Shopping Comparison\" not \"Shopping Skill\").\n\n### Core Optional (what makes a skill useful)\n- **steps**: Numbered, imperative instructions. Use exact tool names. End with a budget step (\"aim for N calls\"). Each step should be one clear action.\n- **tool_deps**: Array of tool names auto-activated on skill load. List ONLY the tools the skill actually uses — fewer tools = lower token footprint in the API request.\n- **description**: One-line summary shown in list_skills results. If the skill covers multiple areas (browsing + spreadsheet), say so explicitly: \"Covers BOTH X and Y — no need to load separate skills.\"\n- **preconditions**: What must be true before using the skill (e.g. \"Cobrowser MCP server connected\").\n- **outcome**: What success looks like when the skill completes.\n\n### Discovery\n- **tags**: Comma-separated. Used by list_skills(tags=[...]) for filtering. Use category tags like \"tool-suite\", \"cobrowser\", \"spreadsheet\", \"amazon\", \"shopping\".\n- **component**: Organizational grouping (e.g. \"cobrowser\", \"spreadsheet\", \"shopping\").\n\n### Optimization Profile\nThe **optimization** field is a JSON dict that tunes runtime behavior when the skill is loaded. All keys are optional — omitted keys use global defaults from preferences.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"context_window\": 8,\n  \"summary_mode\": \"deterministic\",\n  \"tool_idle_turns\": 0,\n  \"tool_budget\": 3000\n}\n```\n\n#### context_window (int)\nHow many recent tool results stay live (full content) in the LLM context. Older results are dehydrated to compact summaries.\n- **Default**: global setting from preferences (typically 5-8)\n- **8**: Good for 10-20 turn tasks. Covers a full extract-then-commit cycle.\n- **12-15**: For longer multi-phase tasks (browse site A, then site B, then compare).\n- **0 or omit**: Use global default.\n\n#### summary_mode (string)\nHow retired tool results are summarized.\n- **\"deterministic\"**: No LLM summary calls. Uses pre-built ToolSummary strings. Zero wall time cost, zero token cost. Best for short tasks (<20 turns) where the dehydrated context is mostly setup (skill loading, policy compliance).\n- **\"llm\"**: Calls the configured summary model. Better quality summaries but adds 20-40s wall time per batch. Best for long sessions where summary quality matters.\n- **Omit**: Use global default from preferences.\n\n#### tool_idle_turns (int)\nEvict tools unused for N turns, regardless of budget. Keeps the tool list lean during long sessions.\n- **0**: Disabled. Use when tool_deps is small (<15 tools) — no need to evict.\n- **7-10**: Good for broad skills with 20+ tools. Evicts tools from earlier phases (e.g. spreadsheet tools during cobrowser extraction).\n- **3**: Too aggressive — tools get evicted before the setup phase completes.\n\n#### tool_budget (int, tokens)\nMax tokens for tool schemas in the API request. Controls how many tools the LLM sees.\n- **3000**: Tight. Good for narrow skills with <12 tools (~1500-2000 tokens).\n- **6000**: Medium. Fits 25-30 tools.\n- **10000**: Generous. Fits 50+ tools. Use when loading multiple broad skills.\n- **Omit**: Use global default (10000).\n\n## Design Principles\n\n### Narrow vs Broad Skills\n- **Narrow composite skills** (e.g. \"Amazon Shopping Comparison\"): bundle tools from multiple domains (cobrowser + spreadsheet) for a specific workflow. 9-12 tools, tight budget, deterministic summary. Best for common repeatable tasks.\n- **Broad generic skills** (e.g. \"Co-Browser Automation\"): cover an entire tool suite with general-purpose guidance. 10-20 tools, larger budget. Best for ad-hoc work where the specific task isn't known in advance.\n- **Prefer narrow** when you know the workflow. The LLM loads one skill instead of two, sees fewer tools, follows tighter instructions, and uses fewer tokens.\n\n### Steps Writing Tips\n- Use exact tool names: \"cobrowser_page_section\" not \"page section tool\"\n- Include CSS selectors or patterns when known: \"selector='.s-main-slot'\"\n- Include recovery patterns: \"If X returns null, do Y. Do NOT do Z.\"\n- End with a budget: \"Budget: 5-7 cobrowser calls, 3-4 spreadsheet calls.\"\n- Reference injected knowledge: \"If navigate returns _injected_knowledge, read the hints BEFORE building your fields map.\"\n\n### tool_deps Sizing\n| tool_deps count | Est. tokens | Budget needed |\n|----------------|-------------|---------------|\n| 5-9            | 1000-1500   | 3000          |\n| 10-15          | 1500-2500   | 4000-6000     |\n| 16-25          | 2500-4500   | 6000-10000    |\n| 25+            | 4500+       | 10000+        |\n\n### Example: Narrow Skill\n```\ntitle: \"Amazon Shopping Comparison\"\ntool_deps: [cobrowser_tab_new, cobrowser_navigate, cobrowser_page_identity,\n            cobrowser_page_section, cobrowser_query_all,\n            minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor, minerva_get_spreadsheet_data,\n            minerva_format_cells, minerva_set_column_width]\noptimization: {context_window: 8, summary_mode: \"deterministic\",\n               tool_idle_turns: 0, tool_budget: 3000}\n```\n9 tools, ~1500 tokens, fits in 3000 budget with room for protected tools."}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19","type":"kb","status":"active","title":"Amazon Search Anti-Dark-Pattern Guide","description":"Mandatory reading before any Amazon product search or purchase recommendation.","created_at":"2026-04-06T04:50:34","updated_at":"2026-04-08T02:30:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","policy","shopping"],"component":"shopping","key":"amazon-anti-dark-patterns","summary":"Amazon listings use dark patterns that exploit LLMs. This guide gives agent-friendly countermeasures that work within a 25-round tool budget.","article":"## Amazon Dark Patterns That Affect LLMs\n\nSellers optimize product descriptions to manipulate LLM recommendations: persuasive copy, fake differentiation, inflated value claims. The default 'Featured' sort is pay-to-play. Sponsored listings are ads. LLMs that read descriptions literally will consistently recommend overpriced products.\n\n## Search Procedure\n\n### 1. Sort Order\nUse 'Best Sellers' or 'Avg. Customer Review' sort. Do NOT use 'Price: Low to High' — it surfaces junk listings ($0.01 items with 0 reviews) that waste your entire tool budget. Do NOT use the default 'Featured' sort — it is pay-to-play.\n\nTo set sort: navigate to the search URL with &s=review-rank (avg review) or &s=exact-aware-popularity-rank (best sellers).\n\n### 2. Skip Sponsored Listings\nIgnore products with 'Sponsored' labels. In cobrowser query_all results, check the text field for 'Sponsored'. Only extract organic (non-sponsored) results.\n\n### 3. Review Count as Quality Signal\nPrefer products with more reviews — they have more signal. Include the star rating and review count in your output so the human can judge. Do NOT set a hard review minimum that causes you to skip all visible results. If the top organic results have few reviews, report them anyway with the review count noted.\n\n### 4. Price Awareness\nCommon commodity price anchors:\n- USB-C cables: ~$8-12\n- HDMI 2.1 cables (6ft): ~$8-12\n- Phone chargers (20W USB-C): ~$10-15\n\nIf a product costs 2x+ the anchor, flag it as 'premium-priced' in your output. Do NOT reject it — just note the price vs anchor so the human can decide.\n\n### 5. Stop Criteria (IMPORTANT)\nExtract the requested number of products (default: 3) from the FIRST page of results. For each product extract: name, price, URL, star rating, review count. Then STOP. Do not scroll for more results, do not compare across pages, do not read individual reviews. If the user wants deeper analysis, they will ask.\n\n## What NOT To Do\n- Do not sort by price ascending (produces junk)\n- Do not read individual reviews (too expensive)\n- Do not calculate NPS or review distributions (too many tool calls)\n- Do not keep scrolling if you already have enough products\n- Do not reject all results because none meet an arbitrary threshold"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6125a8437f89bf48422c6fa95fb0","type":"policy","status":"active","title":"Block Amazon navigation until anti-dark-pattern KB is read","description":"---policy-rule---\n{\"triggers\": [{\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_navigate\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}, {\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_tab_new\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}], \"effect\": \"block\", \"priority\": 100, \"activate_scope\": null, \"context_predicates\": {\"scope_not_active\": \"amazon-kb-read\"}, \"cooldown\": null, \"provenance\": \"user\", \"approval_state\": \"approved\", \"knowledge_ref\": \"019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19\", \"alternatives\": [\"Read the Amazon Anti-Dark-Pattern Guide first: minerva_docket_get id=019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19\", \"After reading, the amazon-kb-read scope will activate and this block will not fire again this session\"]}\n---end-rule---\n\nPrevents any cobrowser navigation to amazon.com until the agent has read the Anti-Dark-Pattern KB. Fires on both cobrowser_navigate and cobrowser_tab_new so that opening a new tab directly to Amazon is also blocked. The block response includes the KB reference so the agent can read it immediately. Once read, the amazon-kb-read scope activates and subsequent Amazon navigations proceed normally.","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-07T00:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","shopping"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6125ce387c76b4baf20f8a155e97","type":"policy","status":"active","title":"Activate amazon-kb-read scope when KB is accessed","description":"---policy-rule---\n{\"tool_pattern\": \"minerva_docket_get\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"id\": \"019d6120d31e\"}, \"effect\": \"scope\", \"priority\": 50, \"activate_scope\": \"amazon-kb-read\", \"scope_max_actions\": 100, \"scope_ttl_ms\": 2147483647, \"context_predicates\": {}, \"cooldown\": null, \"provenance\": \"user\", \"approval_state\": \"approved\", \"knowledge_ref\": \"\", \"alternatives\": []}\n---end-rule---\n\nWhen the agent reads the Amazon Anti-Dark-Pattern KB (via docket_get), this activates the amazon-kb-read scope. This scope satisfies the block policy's scope_not_active predicate, allowing subsequent Amazon navigation to proceed. Scope lasts 100 tool calls or 10 minutes — sufficient for a full browser automation session.","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-07T18:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","shopping"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6f20000000000000000000000001","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Spreadsheet Workflows","description":"How to create, populate, verify, and format spreadsheets in Minerva. Use this when the task involves tables, CSVs, rankings, comparison grids, or spreadsheet output.","created_at":"2026-04-11T14:08:36-07:00","updated_at":"2026-04-12T13:20:00-07:00","tags":["analysis","csv","spreadsheet","tables","tool-suite"],"preconditions":"Minerva spreadsheet tools available through MCP.","steps":"1. If the user wants a spreadsheet, load this skill first instead of guessing tool names.\n2. Gather ALL data BEFORE creating the spreadsheet. Do not create the spreadsheet until you have every row ready to write. Extract first, create and populate second.\n3. Create and populate in one call: minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor with csv_content. This creates the sheet with data already in it.\n4. CSV formatting rules for csv_content:\n   a. Values with commas: wrap in double-quotes. Example: \"Anker Cable, 60W\" becomes a single cell.\n   b. Values with internal double-quotes: escape by doubling them (\"\"). Example: the text 6\" cable becomes \"6\"\" cable\" in CSV.\n   c. Values WITHOUT commas or quotes: do NOT wrap in quotes.\n   d. Prices: write as plain decimal numbers (9.99 not $9.99). Apply number_format=currency afterward.\n   e. Counts: plain integers (79900 not 79,900).\n   f. Only include columns you have data for. If a field is empty for every row, drop the column.\n5. For subsequent bulk data changes, use minerva_update_spreadsheet_data with csv_content. This clears the sheet and replaces all data.\n6. Default formatting -- apply after every create (3 parallel calls):\n   a. Header row: format_cells range=A1:{last_col}1 bg_color=#2C3E50 text_color=#FFFFFF bold=true\n   b. Column widths: set_column_width -- names ~300, prices ~70, counts ~80, short text ~120, URLs ~350\n   c. Currency columns: format_cells range={price_col}2:{price_col}{last_row} number_format=currency\n   No other formatting unless the user explicitly asks.\n7. Verify: call minerva_get_spreadsheet_data after writing. Check that column count matches expectations and no data spilled into extra columns.\n8. Generate the final answer from the verified spreadsheet contents, not from remembered tool outputs.\n9. Do NOT overwrite the spreadsheet with a different schema unless correcting a verified mistake.\n10. Do NOT embed emojis, unicode symbols, or decorative characters in cell data. Ratings should be numbers (4.7). Flags should be text (Premium-priced).\n11. Adding rows: minerva_add_spreadsheet_row appends after the last row containing data. Use minerva_insert_spreadsheet_row with a specific row number if you need precise placement.\n12. For formulas: minerva_set_cell_formula then minerva_recalculate. For repeated patterns: minerva_fill_down.\n13. Charts only when they help: minerva_add_spreadsheet_chart, then minerva_refresh_charts if data changed.\n14. Use targeted cell edits for small corrections and csv_content replacement for full rebuilds.","outcome":"Spreadsheet tasks complete with the right tool surface loaded up front and the final answer grounded in the actual sheet.","tool_deps":["minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor","minerva_update_spreadsheet_data","minerva_get_spreadsheet_data","minerva_set_cell_formula","minerva_fill_down","minerva_recalculate","minerva_format_cells","minerva_set_column_width","minerva_set_row_height","minerva_add_spreadsheet_chart","minerva_refresh_charts","minerva_add_spreadsheet_row","minerva_add_spreadsheet_column","minerva_insert_spreadsheet_row","minerva_insert_spreadsheet_column","minerva_delete_spreadsheet_row","minerva_delete_spreadsheet_column","minerva_list_charts","minerva_get_chart_image","minerva_update_chart"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8ad9047579b59256247bfc129822","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Text Editor","description":"How to create, populate, read, save, rename, and close text/code editor tabs in Minerva. Use this when the task involves writing code, drafting documents, or any plain-text editor surface.","created_at":"2026-04-14T00:40:05-07:00","updated_at":"2026-05-06T23:35:00","tags":["code","editor","files","text"],"preconditions":"Minerva text-editor MCP tools available. (Activated automatically when this skill is loaded — do NOT call minerva_tool_search for them.)","summary":"Activates Minerva's text-editor MCP tools and the path-canonical document tools. Two flows: path-backed (use minerva_doc_*) and scratchpad (use editor_*).","steps":"1. If the user wants to draft, edit, or view code/text in a Minerva tab, load this skill first instead of guessing tool names. The text-editor and document tools auto-activate.\n\n2. Decide up front whether the editor is path-backed or a scratchpad:\n   a. PATH-BACKED (use this whenever the work is for a file on disk): pass file_path on minerva_create_text_editor. Drive content via the path-canonical doc tools — minerva_doc_read / minerva_doc_write / minerva_doc_edit / minerva_doc_save. The editor pane mirrors the buffer automatically.\n   b. SCRATCHPAD / draft: omit file_path. Drive content via minerva_update_editor / minerva_get_editor_content. Persist later with minerva_save_editor (pass file_path on save_editor to bind the tab to a location, i.e. 'save as').\n\n3. PATH-BACKED FLOW:\n   a. minerva_create_text_editor(name=..., content=optional, file_path=ABSOLUTE_PATH).\n   b. Read with minerva_doc_read(path) — the buffer is the source of truth. Use offset/limit for large files.\n   c. Edit a targeted span with minerva_doc_edit(path, old_string, new_string). old_string must be unique. Replace contents wholesale with minerva_doc_write(path, content). Both operate on the buffer; disk is NOT modified yet.\n   d. Flush to disk with minerva_doc_save(path) when done. Use minerva_doc_save_all to flush every dirty buffer at once.\n   e. Verify after a write/edit by calling minerva_doc_read(path) — what's there is what the next save will write.\n\n4. SCRATCHPAD FLOW:\n   a. minerva_create_text_editor(name=..., content=optional) — omit file_path.\n   b. Set content with minerva_update_editor(editor_name, content). Read it back with minerva_get_editor_content(editor_name).\n   c. Persist with minerva_save_editor(editor_name, file_path) — passing file_path on the first save binds the tab to that location. Subsequent minerva_save_editor(editor_name) calls write to the bound path.\n   d. Once a scratchpad is bound to a path, you can switch to the path-backed flow (minerva_doc_*) for further edits if you prefer.\n\n5. Use a clear, unique name for the tab — it is the handle for create / close / list / rename, and for the scratchpad-flow content ops. Avoid generic names like 'Untitled' or 'tab1' that collide with other editors.\n\n6. Use minerva_list_editors to discover open tabs when you do not already know the editor_name (e.g. continuing work across a session). Returns text, graphics, and spreadsheet editors — filter by type if needed.\n\n7. Rename with minerva_rename_editor(editor_name, new_name) when the document's purpose changes or to disambiguate. The editor_name handle changes immediately — use the new name for subsequent calls.\n\n8. Close with minerva_close_editor(editor_name). Pass force=true only when you have already saved or explicitly want to discard unsaved changes; otherwise let the user be prompted.\n\n9. Do NOT re-run minerva_tool_search for create_text_editor / doc_* / update_editor / get_editor_content / save_editor / list_editors / rename_editor / close_editor — they are activated by this skill for the rest of the session.","outcome":"Text editor tasks complete with content correctly written, optionally persisted to disk, and verified from the live buffer or editor — not from remembered tool outputs.","tool_deps":["minerva_create_text_editor","minerva_doc_read","minerva_doc_write","minerva_doc_edit","minerva_doc_save","minerva_doc_save_all","minerva_update_editor","minerva_get_editor_content","minerva_save_editor","minerva_list_editors","minerva_rename_editor","minerva_close_editor"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8ae63d8f78c830d93591926592cf","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Skill Authoring","description":"How to author a Minerva skill at runtime — field contract, tool_deps sizing, and optimization knobs. Uses minerva_skill_create which handles insertion, active-status transition, and tool activation in one call.","created_at":"2026-04-14T00:40:05-07:00","updated_at":"2026-04-15T00:00:00-07:00","tags":["authoring","meta","skills"],"preconditions":"minerva_skill_create is available.","summary":"Runtime recipe for writing a skill that auto-activates its tools — one tool call, no file editing.","steps":"1. Clarify with the user: narrow composite (one workflow, 5-12 tools, tight budget) vs broad generic (an entire tool suite, 15-25 tools, larger budget). Prefer narrow when the workflow is known.\n\n2. Draft the skill's contract on paper first:\n   a. **title** — domain + action, unique in the project. Short — appears in the skill chooser.\n   b. **description** — one line that appears in skill_list results. Lead with when the skill should be used.\n   c. **preconditions** — what must already be true (MCP server connected, docket loaded, etc.).\n   d. **outcome** — what success looks like after the skill completes.\n   e. **steps** — numbered imperative actions. Use EXACT tool names, not paraphrases.\n   f. **tool_deps** — every MCP tool the skill might call. Auto-activation reads this.\n   g. **tags** — category filters for skill_list queries (e.g. \"editor\", \"spreadsheet\", \"cobrowser\").\n\n3. Steps-writing rules:\n   a. Number every step. One imperative action per step.\n   b. Use exact tool names: `minerva_create_text_editor`, not \"the text editor tool\".\n   c. Include known selectors / flags / literal args (\"use delay=3 for JS hydration\").\n   d. Include recovery patterns: \"If X returns null, do Y. Do NOT do Z.\"\n   e. End with a call budget: \"Aim for 5-7 tool calls; reconsider if past 10.\"\n   f. Last step: \"Do NOT re-run minerva_tool_search for <these tools> — they are activated by this skill.\"\n\n4. Size tool_deps deliberately. Every tool adds schema bytes to every request. Canonical bands:\n   a. 5-9 deps → ~1000-1500 tokens → tool_budget ≈ 3000.\n   b. 10-15 deps → ~1500-2500 tokens → tool_budget ≈ 4000-6000.\n   c. 16-25 deps → ~2500-4500 tokens → tool_budget ≈ 6000-10000.\n   d. Drop any tool the steps never name — no aspirational tools.\n\n5. Optional **optimization** profile fields (omit any key to fall back to global defaults):\n   - `context_window` (int) — live tool result window. 8 for 10-20 turn tasks, 12-15 for multi-phase.\n   - `summary_mode` — \"deterministic\" for short tasks (free, zero wall time); \"llm\" for long sessions where summary quality matters.\n   - `tool_idle_turns` (int) — evict tools unused for N turns. 0 when tool_deps < 15; 7-10 for broad skills.\n   - `tool_budget` (int, tokens) — see sizing table in step 4.\n\n6. Call `minerva_skill_create(title, description, steps, preconditions, outcome, tool_deps, tags, optimization)`. Status defaults to \"active\" and every entry in tool_deps is auto-activated.\n\n7. Verify with `minerva_get_skill(title=<name>)`. The response must include `\"activated_tools\": [...]` listing every entry from tool_deps. If some are missing, inspect `skipped_tools` in the skill_create response — those tool names were not found in the tool index. Fix the names and re-create.","outcome":"A new skill lives in the docket, status=active, and minerva_get_skill returns a populated activated_tools list matching tool_deps.","tool_deps":["minerva_skill_create","minerva_list_skills","minerva_get_skill","minerva_docket_skill_list"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a11dd8c8c0741c8f244bfc056d7fbb","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Engineering Process","description":"Turn authorized goals into reviewed changes and measured results.","created_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:28","updated_at":"2026-10-09T07:04:21","tags":["engineering","engineering-process","orchestration","process","review"],"preconditions":"Read the goal, repository instructions, language skills and user settings; defaults do not widen authority.","steps":"## 1. Simple and owned\n\nOne owner per rule; the simplest working shape.\n\n- Search before adding policy. Extend its owner through public contracts and plain values; views retain presentation and interaction, owners retain reusable decisions.\n- Refactor substantial shared logic without changing results, then change behaviour in a separately reviewable step. DRY is useful when it does not buy much complexity.\n- Keep files around 1–2 KLOC: refactor when crossing 2,000 lines; explain growth of an already oversized file. Names show intent; owners document their rules; current comments explain why, not project history.\n\n## 2. Determinism first\n\nMake work mechanical, rerunnable, verifiable and convergent.\n\n- Keep one authoritative definition. Use diffs, schemas and independent oracles instead of hand transcription or remembered procedure.\n- Let software own IDs, timing, routing, retries, deduplication and routine receipts; make defaults and failures explicit.\n- Tools expose capabilities. Compose the engineering process outside them; CI, build and test-only machinery belongs outside shipped product code.\n\n## 3. Size batches; ceremony scales with risk\n\nBatch size controls velocity; risk, never diff size, sets ceremony.\n\n- A batch is the work one implementation agent does in one pass. Break the goal into tasks with their own acceptance checks, then group tasks into batches. Too small and process overhead (reviews, tests, handoffs) dominates; too large and the implementer loses context and bugs creep in. Keep each batch within one tier.\n- Heavy: data, migrations, secrets, crypto and keys. Standard: user-visible, tool or contract changes, release and version guards. Light: docs, renames, fixtures, diagnostics and deletion of demonstrably replaced code.\n- Default budgets of agent run-time, excluding user waits: Light ≈45 minutes, Standard ≈2 hours, Heavy ≈4 hours. Use existing tier profiles; split unreadable batches.\n- Unpushed **OR** one revert away lowers one tier, except data and secrets. Raise it for new risk or persistent unexplained failures; lower it for proven fixture-only causes.\n- At the limit, stop incomplete and name the cause: scope, estimate, environment, process or a deeper cause found. Cut or re-estimate with the user, or within a standing delegation the user has given; never silently add rounds.\n\n## 4. Stay in scope\n\nEvery admitted item serves a stated goal or an explicit user safety decision.\n\n- Name goals, non-goals, touch-set and acceptance observations. Write acceptance checks a lazy implementation would fail; measure passing goal checks, not an empty queue.\n- Park unrelated discoveries without losing them. A nearby fix is admitted only as a goal blocker or a small obvious fix in the same area that adds no behaviour, fits the budget and needs no product choice.\n- Track every unresolved finding with blast-radius priority 1–4; deferral never lowers it. P1 harms the machine, is externally irreversible or loses third-party data; P4 is one loud path or regenerable own data.\n- A changed goal reopens approval. While waiting for an answer, continue independent authorized work; dependent work waits for the actual answer.\n\n## 5. Evidence over claims\n\nTie behavioural claims to evidence from the candidate.\n\n- Establish facts from code or commands; record revision, conditions and measurement age. Read logs as well as exit status. Missing, skipped or failed checks are not passes.\n- Compare candidate and baseline diagnostics under the same tools and configuration: project-relative file + code + normalized message and count, ignoring shifted line numbers. Preserve logs; report a manual comparison honestly.\n- Replacing code invalidates earlier proof. Pin an authorized commit before final review and expensive runs and report only what its checks established.\n- If stuck: ask whether the answer matters, try a mechanical check, provide pointers, narrow the question, then use existing tool-equipped agents. Do not build new machinery to avoid the stop.\n\n## 6. Independent eyes\n\nSeparate writer and reviewer; independence needs fresh context and access.\n\n- Serialize writers, including fixers. Reviewers start fresh with source access, a different model when available, the same goal, constraints and candidate, and no writer explanations or other initial reviews.\n- DEFECT blocks when an existing reachable path is wrong or unsafe. RULE blocks unless a commit exception or the user or designated orchestrator justifies its disposition; cite the rule and file:line. SUGGESTION never blocks.\n- Judge blockers against the admitted goal, contracts and safety; park unrelated pre-existing findings. Likely supported paths matter more than imagined possibilities; do not dismiss a demonstrated data or secret defect because it is rare.\n- New guards are optional unless the accepted design requires them. Record every disposition and review the actual repair; report capability gaps instead of claiming review happened.\n\n## 7. Decide by rubric and record why\n\nDecide within authority; escalate choices only the user can settle.\n\n- Use Engineering Decisions: the user's designated rubric overrides the ten-axis default, with Determinism first.\n- Validate premises, include doing nothing, compare options, and record the decisive axis, chosen default and falsifier. Keep cheap reversible choices concise.\n- Escalate design choices only for product identity, unsettled user-visible behaviour, or no safe option. Record a user ruling as a ruling, not a peer's new permission.\n\n## 8. Test where it counts\n\nUse few broad tests that can falsify the change.\n\n- Exercise real paths and independent outcomes; avoid mocks and internal seams as substitutes for required real coverage. Tests use public contracts and constants; humans remain the oracle for perceptual acceptance.\n- Static checks and focused tests may run while implementing. Expensive container and integration jobs and the full suite follow final review on the exact candidate.\n- Light uses compile and focused tests. Standard and Heavy run one full suite on the commit to push; cold reruns belong only to that candidate.\n- Fix forward; chase failures with focused tests, re-review repairs, and keep at most three fix, review and test rounds. A replacement candidate needs current final evidence. Never weaken tests to get green; the cap ends incomplete.\n\n## 9. No surprises\n\nProtect the live workspace, data and publishing authority; make failures visible.\n\n- Test and debug on an isolated, disposable machine (a container or VM) instead of the dev box: reproducible environments protect live work and reproduce failures. Isolate project and profile copies; validate inputs and recover visibly.\n- Deletes driven by a script or variable need independent review before execution. Stop for new machinery, changed permissions or sandbox or dropping data or secret checks.\n- Source-build or fetch executable dependencies from official or known channels; no foreign checked-in binaries. Scan the exact outgoing history for secrets, including those removed in later commits.\n- The human owns push and release authority. Make the candidate and evidence concrete before approval; reuse existing approval within scope. Peer review cannot grant it. Record CI on the pushed revision and fix failures forward.\n\n## 10. Track what lasts\n\nKeep decisions recoverable and temporary coordination temporary.\n\n- Create a Docket item before every change; questions are read-only. Commits cite item IDs; follow repository conventions, otherwise end with `Rules: <IDs or ideas>; exceptions: <none or reason>`.\n- Use owning APIs for tool-managed files. Durable decisions, criteria, outcomes and lessons belong in ordinary Docket items.\n- Temporary coordination, progress and raw reports go in Docket ephemeral items, kept only if promoted. Hand off pointers; keep code, large diffs and private evidence outside Docket bodies.\n- Close only when required acceptance and checks actually pass. Human checks remain human gates.\n","outcome":"Goal-directed work, bounded effort, independent review and traceable results.","source":"master"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a11dd9273a76c690a1d9d57c472b5d","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Engineering Decisions","description":"Verify a problem, compare real options and record a measured choice with a falsifier.","created_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:53","updated_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:53","tags":["decisions","engineering","engineering-decisions","rubric"],"preconditions":"A tracked goal, authorized scope and source and evidence access. Read Engineering Process and any user-designated rubric.","steps":"## Default rubric\n\nA user's designated rubric item overrides this default. Grade every axis High, Medium or Low; Cost uses S, M or L (default changed-code estimate: S ≤100, M 101–1,000, L >1,000 lines). **Determinism is first and normally wins a conflict**; do not hide the decisive distinction in an arithmetic total.\n\n| Axis, in order | Question |\n|---|---|\n| 0. Determinism | One machine-readable owner? Mechanical, rerunnable, verifiable by a diff or oracle and convergent after upstream edits? Loud refusal beats guessing. |\n| 1. Reliability | What is most likely to work now? |\n| 2. Durability | What resists drift, follows standards and assumes less? |\n| 3. Performance | What costs less memory, CPU, GPU or network? |\n| 4. Debuggability | Can someone trace the chain and fix the next bug? |\n| 5. LLM Ergonomics | Compact validated interface and recovery; software owns routine timing, routing, retries and receipts without hiding authority or failure? |\n| 6. DRY | One authoritative rule or implementation without costly abstraction? |\n| 7. Cost | Honest S, M or L estimate including integration and upkeep? |\n| 8. Discoverable | Clear naming, expected location and obvious intent? |\n| 9. User-visible | Does the user see that something failed and what? Silence or empty output scores low. |\n\nWeighting example: a user may give Rust a large **Reliability** boost over GDScript and weigh Debuggability and Discoverable heavily. State that preference, then assess actual integration and costs; it does not authorize a rewrite.\n\n## Short decider procedure\n\n1. **Validate the problem.** Check current code and behaviour. Correct a false premise; an already-solved or unconfirmed problem can end the investigation successfully.\n2. **Sketch options, including doing nothing or deferring.** Identify the load-bearing claims that distinguish them before doing broad research.\n3. **Establish claims from code or measured commands.** Cite symbol and path, revision, conditions and age. Prose, hints and reports are claims to verify about behaviour; user requirements are requirements. Mark unmeasured facts instead of scoring guesses as evidence.\n4. **Reshape and score.** Present one column per option and one row per axis. Trace consequential scores to claims. Cheap reversible choices need a compact comparison, not a research project.\n5. **Record one recommendation or no recommendation.** Name the decisive axis and **falsifier**: the fact that would change the answer. Record consequential rationale and table in the tracker, along with open facts and who may veto the default. A ratified user ruling is recorded as such.\n\nUse `Axis | Option A | Option B | Do nothing`, then: `Choose <option>; decisive axis <axis>, because <measured distinction>; falsifier <fact>.` The null option is a real choice, not an afterthought.\n\nEscalate design judgment only for **product identity**, **unsettled user-visible behaviour**, or **no safe option**. Engineering Process separately governs scope, budgets and permission stops; this skill widens no authority.\n\nDeletion needs positive evidence: a named replacement or recorded decision. \"No current production caller\" describes today; it does not prove future irrelevance, and test infrastructure is not dead code.\n","outcome":"A reasoned default or a precise escalation, recorded where the next person can act on it.","source":"master"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a11dd9299273f89b4a8a4805926fae","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Engineering: GDScript","description":"Use for Godot/GDScript changes alongside Engineering Process. Keep reusable rules independent of scenes, pass dependencies explicitly and type what is knowable.","created_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:53","updated_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:53","tags":["engineering","engineering-gdscript","gdscript","godot"],"preconditions":"Read Engineering Process and repository instructions. Identify the project's required Godot editor version and its documented checking/test environment.","steps":"## Steps\n\nEngineering Process owns the workflow: focused iteration, independent review, and expensive final runs on the reviewed candidate. The steps below describe language-specific work, not a separate execution order. Use Engineering Decisions for trade-offs.\n\n1. Read Engineering Process and locate the existing rule owner. Separate reusable policy from scene orchestration and presentation.\n2. Apply G1–G4 and GD-1–GD-3 below. Preserve existing contracts and serialization behaviour during extraction.\n3. Check each changed script with the project's matching editor binary in the documented project context. Use isolated copies/profiles where a running application shares the checkout or user data. Runtime tests must use the repository's safe test procedure.\n4. Run existing relevant tests and required diagnostic gates. Read diagnostics as well as exit status; Godot can report script errors while returning zero. If a standalone check lacks autoload context, use the repository's project-aware procedure rather than dismissing arbitrary errors.\n5. Follow Engineering Process for independent review, fixes and the commit declaration. Do not suppress a warning merely to make the gate pass.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **G1 Pure rule logic** belongs in `RefCounted` classes or static functions on a `class_name` script, without `Node`, `get_tree()`, `$` or global service-locator access. Orchestration that needs node lifecycle may be a `Node`, delegating rules to their owners.\n- **G2 Prefer scenes** to UI generated in code. Scene scripts follow R3: presentation and interaction belong there; reusable policy belongs to an owner.\n- **G3 Type everything knowable:** variables, parameters and returns. Use `Variant`, untyped containers or an `as` cast only for genuinely dynamic values. In isolated tests, classes depending on unavailable autoload context may need runtime `load()` and locals that cannot be statically resolved may remain untyped; document the reason.\n- **G4 Pass dependencies in.** New code receives its dependencies through parameters or initialization instead of reaching through global service chains. Existing patterns follow repository exceptions; changing a nearby line does not authorize a broad migration.\n- **GD-1 JSON numeric boundaries.** Godot's JSON parser represents numbers as floats. Where the contract requires an integer, validate and convert with `int()` at the boundary. Keep fractional values where the contract requires them. Verify with a JSON round trip, not only a literal dictionary.\n- **GD-2 Plugin class names.** Prefix a plugin's global `class_name` names with its plugin prefix so separate plugins cannot collide.\n- **GD-3 Safe syntax checking.** Use the project context and matching editor binary. Never launch another instance against a live checkout/profile without the user's authorization; imports and runtime tests use isolated copies and the repository's documented procedure.\n\n## Check commands\n\nFor each changed script, with paths adjusted to the project:\n\n```sh\ngodot --headless --path PROJECT --check-only --script res://path/to/script.gd\n```\n\nRun the repository's existing relevant tests and warning comparison procedure, or apply the diagnostic-comparison guidance in Engineering Process. Do not assume `--check-only` collects every editor analyzer warning or supplies every autoload dependency. A missing/wrong editor version is a named prerequisite failure, not permission to skip the checks.\n\nDeclare applicable G1–G4 and GD-1–GD-3 IDs using Engineering Process's commit format.\n","outcome":"GDScript changes satisfy the G/GD rules, pass relevant syntax and repository checks, and declare any justified exceptions.","source":"master"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a11dd92adf7058a9eb80f24b933d8c","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Engineering: Python","description":"Use for Python changes alongside Engineering Process. Type public boundaries, keep pure rules separate from I/O, propagate errors visibly and inject dependencies.","created_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:53","updated_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:54","tags":["engineering","engineering-python","python"],"preconditions":"Read Engineering Process and repository instructions. Use the repository's approved Python environment, test commands and Ruff configuration/version.","steps":"## Steps\n\nEngineering Process owns the workflow: focused iteration, independent review, and expensive final runs on the reviewed candidate. The steps below describe language-specific work, not a separate execution order. Use Engineering Decisions for trade-offs.\n\n1. Read Engineering Process and find the existing owner and callers of each changed rule.\n2. Apply PY-1–PY-5 below. Preserve behaviour when extracting shared logic before adding the new use.\n3. Compile changed files, run Ruff including ANN typing rules, and run the repository's existing relevant tests. Compare diagnostics against the pre-change code with the same tool version and configuration; retain existing diagnostics visibly rather than treating them as new defects or silently ignoring them.\n4. If a required checker is unavailable, report the prerequisite and the repository-approved installation path. Do not mark skipped checks passed or silently install tools into an unrelated/global environment.\n5. Follow Engineering Process for independent review, fixes and the commit declaration.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **PY-1 Type the boundaries.** Public functions have parameter and return annotations. Known structured payloads crossing module boundaries use a `dataclass` or `TypedDict`, not an unspecified bare `dict`. Genuinely dynamic data remains explicitly identified as dynamic.\n- **PY-2 Pure logic stays pure.** Rule logic uses plain functions/classes without network, file or process I/O. I/O lives at the edges and calls the rule owners.\n- **PY-3 No silent failures.** No bare `except:` or `except Exception: pass`. Catch the specific error and recover visibly or re-raise with context.\n- **PY-4 Pass dependencies in.** Clients, sessions and paths are parameters or constructor arguments, not module-level dependencies created at import time.\n- **PY-5 Standard library first.** Name a new third-party dependency and its reason in the commit. Reuse the repository's existing approved dependencies where appropriate.\n\n## Check commands\n\nRun in the approved environment, substituting the changed files:\n\n```sh\npython3 -m py_compile path/to/changed.py\npython3 -m ruff check --extend-select ANN path/to/changed.py\n```\n\nAlso run the existing tests for the touched module. Apply the diagnostic-comparison guidance in Engineering Process. Ruff is required for the annotation check; if missing, give an explicit installation hint for the approved environment. An example is `python3 -m pip install ruff` when that environment and installation are authorized. Respect repository pins rather than assuming the newest release.\n\nDeclare applicable PY-1–PY-5 IDs using Engineering Process's commit format.\n","outcome":"Python changes satisfy PY-1–PY-5, pass relevant syntax/lint/tests and declare any justified exceptions.","source":"master"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a11dd92c2e711bb29692d8a6773ae9","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Engineering: Go","description":"Use for Go changes alongside Engineering Process. Preserve error chains, define small interfaces at consumers, inject state and honour cancellation.","created_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:54","updated_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:54","tags":["engineering","engineering-go","go"],"preconditions":"Read Engineering Process and repository instructions. Identify the affected Go modules/packages and their approved toolchain and test commands.","steps":"## Steps\n\nEngineering Process owns the workflow: focused iteration, independent review, and expensive final runs on the reviewed candidate. The steps below describe language-specific work, not a separate execution order. Use Engineering Decisions for trade-offs.\n\n1. Read Engineering Process and identify the rule owner, its consumers and the affected modules.\n2. Apply GO-1–GO-5 below. Keep behaviour-preserving extraction separately reviewable when substantial.\n3. Format changed files. Run `go vet` in the affected modules and the relevant package tests. Compare any diagnostics against the pre-change baseline using the same toolchain and configuration.\n4. Check cancellation and error propagation on the paths changed, using existing tests or focused cases where those behaviours matter. Do not add interfaces or generic frameworks only for hypothetical callers.\n5. Follow Engineering Process for independent review, fixes and the commit declaration.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **GO-1 Errors are values.** Do not discard errors with `_`. Wrap errors with context while preserving the chain, for example `fmt.Errorf(\"load %s: %w\", name, err)`. Reserve `panic` for programmer errors.\n- **GO-2 Small interfaces, defined by the consumer.** Accept interfaces and return concrete types. Do not introduce an interface for a single implementation unless an actual test needs the substitution.\n- **GO-3 No package-level mutable state.** Receive dependencies through constructors or parameters.\n- **GO-4 Context first.** Operations that block or perform I/O take `ctx context.Context` as their first parameter and honour cancellation.\n- **GO-5 No speculative generics.** Use type parameters when two concrete types need the abstraction now.\n\n## Check commands\n\nAdjust paths to the changed files/packages; run module commands from each affected module:\n\n```sh\ngofmt -l path/to/changed.go\ngo vet ./...\ngo test ./path/to/affected/package\n```\n\n`gofmt -l` must print nothing; use `gofmt -w path/to/changed.go` to fix reported formatting, then check again. Apply the diagnostic-comparison guidance in Engineering Process and the repository's existing test command where it differs. Do not silently change module requirements or download a different toolchain just to bypass an incompatibility.\n\nDeclare applicable GO-1–GO-5 IDs using Engineering Process's commit format.\n","outcome":"Go changes satisfy GO-1–GO-5, are formatted, pass relevant vet/tests and declare any justified exceptions.","source":"master"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a11dd92dec772493919b35d35ebd24","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Engineering: Rust","description":"Use for Rust changes alongside Engineering Process. Handle fallible input explicitly, keep library errors typed, justify unsafe code and avoid speculative abstractions.","created_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:54","updated_at":"2026-10-08T23:28:54","tags":["engineering","engineering-rust","rust"],"preconditions":"Read Engineering Process and repository instructions. Identify the affected crates, approved toolchain and relevant feature/test configurations.","steps":"## Steps\n\nEngineering Process owns the workflow: focused iteration, independent review, and expensive final runs on the reviewed candidate. The steps below describe language-specific work, not a separate execution order. Use Engineering Decisions for trade-offs.\n\n1. Read Engineering Process and find the existing rule owner and crate consumers.\n2. Apply RS-1–RS-5 below. Preserve behaviour during substantial extraction before changing the rule.\n3. Run formatting, Clippy and relevant tests for the changed crates using the repository's feature configuration. Compare Clippy warnings with the pre-change code under the same toolchain/features; do not clean unrelated old warnings or hide new ones.\n4. Review changed error, ownership and unsafe paths against their actual callers and invariants. Record justified exceptions instead of inventing abstractions for future uses.\n5. Follow Engineering Process for independent review, fixes and the commit declaration.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **RS-1 No panics on input.** No `unwrap()` or `expect()` on input, I/O or network results, except in tests. An infallible case carries a comment explaining why it is infallible.\n- **RS-2 Typed errors in libraries.** Library code exposes its own error type. Binaries may collect failures at their boundary and report them.\n- **RS-3 Unsafe needs a reason.** Every `unsafe` block has a `// SAFETY:` comment stating the invariant that makes the operation safe.\n- **RS-4 Simple ownership first.** Prefer owned values and plain functions. Add lifetimes, generics or traits when an actual second caller needs them, rather than speculating. A lifetime required for an existing function's correct borrowing is not speculative abstraction.\n- **RS-5 No silenced lints.** An `#[allow(...)]` needs a comment explaining its reason. An uncommented allowance counts as gaming a gate under R10.\n\n## Check commands\n\nRun in each affected crate/workspace with the repository's relevant package/features:\n\n```sh\ncargo fmt --check\ncargo clippy\ncargo test\n```\n\nScope tests to affected packages/behaviours where the repository allows it. Apply the diagnostic-comparison guidance in Engineering Process. Formatting must pass and Clippy must introduce no new warnings. Preserve the distinction between a compiler/lint failure and an unavailable prerequisite; neither is a successful check.\n\nDeclare applicable RS-1–RS-5 IDs using Engineering Process's commit format.\n","outcome":"Rust changes satisfy RS-1–RS-5, pass formatting, Clippy and relevant tests, and declare any justified exceptions.","source":"master"}
